    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ibn Sa'id al-Maghribi placed the capital Wafāt on high ground in a river valley where the people grew bananas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">al-Umari</a> counted seven cities, an army of 20,000 soldiers and 15,000 horsemen, and a ruler who wore headbands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>.</li><li>Sabr ad-Din's rebellion of 1332 aimed to make him king of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, turn the churches into mosques and set the Christian queen to grind corn; Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a>'s capital, looted his camp of gold and slaves, imprisoned him and installed his brother Jamal ad-Din I.</li><li>Haqq ad-Din II moved the capital to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> plateau and fought the emperor in more than twenty battles before dying in 1376; his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saad-ad-din-ii">Sa'ad ad-Din II</a> was defeated in 1403 and killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>, after which, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a> wrote, 'the strength of the Muslims was abated'.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon</a> threatened in 1320 to divert the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and starve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> unless the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a> stopped persecuting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> dismissed it as an idle threat; the fear that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> might tamper with the Nile nonetheless stayed with them for centuries.</p></aside>
    </section>

