    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yekuno-amlak"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yekuno-amlak.jpg" alt="Yekuno Amlak" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yekuno-amlak">Yekuno Amlak</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yekuno_Amlak.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> came to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">hijra</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>'s two-mihrab mosque, the Masjid al-Qiblatayn, dates from about the seventh century and is among the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>; in the late ninth century al-Yaqubi described Muslims living along the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> seaboard and Zeila as a self-governing trading port whose merchants shipped goods to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Inland, the Sultanate of Shewa is believed to have been the first Muslim state of the interior; by its chronicle the conversion of its people began in 1108 with the Gbbah.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walashma dynasty</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a>. Umar ibn Dunya-huz, later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-walasma">Umar Walasma</a>, carved out a kingdom of his own and conquered the Sultanate of Shewa; in 1288 Sultan Wali Asma imposed his rule on Hubat, Zeila and the other Muslim states of the region, a consolidation of the Muslim lands that Taddesse Tamrat compares with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yekuno-amlak">Yekuno Amlak</a>'s consolidation of the Christian highlands in the same years. The dynasty claimed Arab descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> through Aqil ibn Abi Talib, a lineage most probably invented for prestige: the Walashma were most likely of local origin. By <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a>'s account Umar died around 1275 and 'four or five sons' ruled briefly until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabr-ad-din-i">Sabr ad-Din I</a> held power to the turn of the century; his successor Sultan Ali was the first to war with Abyssinia and soon submitted again for want of popular support.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The earliest account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a> is Ibn Sa'id al-Maghribi's: the region was called Jabarta and its capital Wafāt, a city on high ground in a valley beside a river, on the eastern edge of Shewa, whose Muslim inhabitants of mixed descent grew bananas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">Al-Umari</a> placed the state near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast, fifteen days' travel by twenty, with a river (the Awash) and a large population, and named seven cities, Biqulzar, Kwelgora, Shimi, Shewa, Adal, Jamme and Laboo. His account implies a land 300 by 400 kilometres, which Richard Pankhurst thinks exaggerated. Five ruined towns of the fourteenth century, Asbäri, Nora, Mäsal, Rassa Guba and Beri-Ifat, have been found in eastern Shewa, their houses resembling Argobba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harari">Harari</a> building.</p><p>Wealth came through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>. The port, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-el-mandeb">Bab al-Mandab</a> strait, was a node of transcontinental trade and the most important entry point for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> into the Ethiopian lands, and Ifat's rulers controlled it as a commercial and religious base. The people were diverse: nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somalis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afars</a> and Warjih, already Muslim by the thirteenth century, the Silte, Hararis and Argobbas, and the now extinct Doba and Harla. Arabic was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a>, but the inhabitants spoke Cushitic and Ethio-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a>. Khat, forbidden to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christians, was widely chewed by Muslims at Mar'ade, and the ruler of Ifat wore headbands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i.jpg" alt="Amda Seyon I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amda_Seyon1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">Al-Umari</a> credited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a> with an army of 20,000 soldiers and 15,000 horsemen; its infantry, according to Mohammed Hassen, were the Argobba. Its wars were with the Christian empire. In 1320 Haqq ad-Din I invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> kingdom, burnt churches, forced Christians to apostatise, seized the emperor's envoy on his way back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and killed him when he would not convert; the emperor raided all Shewa, then largely Muslim, and the districts of Ifat. His son Dadader Haqq ad-Din fell at the battle of Marra Biete. In early 1332 Sabr ad-Din divided his troops in three, sending one division against Amhara, one against Angot and leading the third into Shewa.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon</a> paid his soldiers in gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and fine clothes, yet many would not march into Ifat's arid, roadless mountains; those who did advanced on 24 Yakatit, put the sultan to flight, destroyed his capital, killed many of his soldiers and sacked a camp, taking its people as slaves. Sabr ad-Din surrendered and was imprisoned. Haqq ad-Din II fought the emperor ceaselessly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> plateau and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>, in more than twenty battles to 1370, and died in battle in 1376; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saad-ad-din-ii">Sa'ad ad-Din II</a> waged hit-and-run raids and struck chiefs loyal to the emperor at Zalan and Hadeya, until the imperial army defeated Ifat on the Harar plateau in 1403. A court hymn for Emperor Yishaq's victory over the sultan's troops holds the first known mention of the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad.jpg" alt="al-Nasir Muhammad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maqamat_of_al-Hariri._Enthroned_Prince._Probably_Egypt_1334.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">14th century authors, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a> was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walashma</a> sultans from their capital in eastern Shewa, and later from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> plateau and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>. The states of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> that had preceded it, Gidaya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawaro">Dawaro</a>, Sawans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> and Fatagar, were incorporated into the sultanate but kept a measure of independence; Taddesse Tamrat counts Fatagar, Dawaro and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bale">Bale</a> within its borders, and Fatagar stayed under Ifat's control after the rest were invaded. Ifat kept its own diplomacy: it sent an embassy under Shaykh Abdallah al-Zayla'i to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><p>After 1332 the sultans were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Christian emperor, who claimed the right to make them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon</a> imprisoned Sabr ad-Din and appointed his brother Jamal ad-Din I; his son Sayfa Arad named Ahmad, Harb Arad ibn Ali, sultan and jailed Ali's father and relatives, but Ahmad was killed in an Ifat uprising and his son Haqq ad-Din II won the throne through a series of battles against his kinsman Mola Asfah. Tegulat, once the capital of the Shewa sultanate and known to Muslims as Mar'ade, became Amda Seyon's own seat, and the emperor appointed the Walasma descendants king of all the Muslim lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> governed the state: the chroniclers of Amda Seyon treat Sabr ad-Din's war, with its governors appointed for Fatagar, Alamalé, Damot, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a>, Angot, Inderta, Begemder and Gojjam and its threat to plant khat at the capital, as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> rather than a bid for independence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harar.jpg" alt="Harar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_Gate,_Harar_Jugol_(14464345823).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rod Waddington from Kergunyah, Australia, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a> was the northernmost of the Muslim states of the Horn and the buffer between the Christian kingdom and the Muslim states of the coast. Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a> it was the most important entry point for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> into the Ethiopian lands, and under its rule the faith spread: by the middle of the fourteenth century the Muslim peoples north of the Awash were under the sultanate. Its wars were also part of a wider contest. Mordechai Abir ties them to the conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>' persecution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a> provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon</a>'s threats in 1320, the arrest of Patriarch Marcos in 1352 led Ethiopia to imprison every Egyptian merchant, and only in 1361 did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-Malik al-Salih free the patriarch and seek friendship with the emperor.</p><p>The sultanate's fall shaped the region's next two centuries. Haqq ad-Din II's removal of the capital to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> plateau makes him for some the true founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>, which emerged with Harar as its capital as the leading Muslim principality of the south-east in the later fifteenth century. By <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harari">Harari</a> tradition the Argobba fled Ifat for the Aw Abdal lowlands around Harar during the fifteenth-century war. Smaller territories went on being ruled by Walasma groups until the eighteenth century, when Christian dynasties named Yifat and Menz, after the sultanate's provinces, arose in their place; the name survives in the Ethiopian district of Yifat in North Shewa of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> region.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saad-ad-din-ii">Sa'ad ad-Din II</a>, who succeeded his brother in 1376, kept up the attacks on the Abyssinian Christian army and on the chiefs who supported the emperor at Zalan and Hadeya in hit-and-run raids. Early in the fifteenth century the emperor, probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawit-i">Dawit I</a>, gathered a large army, branded the Muslims of the region 'enemies of the Lord' and invaded. After much fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a>'s troops were defeated on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> plateau in 1403, and the sultan fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a> with Ethiopian soldiers in pursuit. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">Al-Maqrizi</a> relates that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> besieged him on the peninsula of Zeila, cut off his water, were shown a way to him 'by one of the impious', and after three days of battle wounded him in the forehead and pierced him with their swords, so that 'he died happily, falling in God's cause'.</p><p>No other contemporary source confirms the invasion of Zeila, and an Amhara incursion there is thought unlikely; another contemporary source dates Sa'ad ad-Din's death to 1410 and credits Emperor Yeshaq with killing him. After his death, al-Maqrizi wrote, 'the strength of the Muslims was abated', the Amhara settled the country and made churches of the ravaged mosques, and the followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> were harassed for more than twenty years. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walashma</a> survived in Adal, whose capital Harar led the Muslims of the south-east from the later fifteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a>, ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walashma</a> from eastern Shewa to the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>, consolidated the Muslim lands of the Horn in the late thirteenth century and fought the Christian emperors from 1320. Crushed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon</a> in 1332 and defeated on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> plateau in 1403, it gave way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> at Harar.</em></p>

